## PHI hygiene (non-negotiable)

This engagement touches PHI under PHIPA. Audit and traceability rest on the discipline encoded here. Every role enforces these.

- **No real client/patient names, IDs, addresses, DOBs, health card numbers, or SINs in chat, prompts, specs, code, tests, or commit messages.** Use ticket IDs (FDE-XXXX), FR IDs (FR-XXXX:AC-Y), and synthetic identifiers ("Patient A", "Provider 1") instead.
- **Test fixtures use synthetic data only.** Generated, not derived from real records. Fixtures live in `tests/fixtures/` and are reviewed for hygiene before commit.
- **Real-data testing happens in AlayaCare staging environments**, not via Claude Code sessions. If you need to verify against real records, exit Claude Code, run the verification manually in the staging tool, and bring back only the *outcome* (pass/fail/observation) — not the data.
- **Escalations cite IDs, not details.** "FR-0007:AC-2 ambiguous on retention behavior" — not "the issue with the record for [name]".
- **If you encounter real PHI in a file you're asked to edit**, stop, do not commit, flag to the human supervisor. The PHI regex hook will also catch obvious patterns and block the write — treat any block as a hard stop, not a thing to work around.

## Multi-agent execution model — Pattern 2 (Hybrid)

This engagement uses three role-specialized contexts, separated by session boundary:

- **Architect** — runs in a dedicated Claude Code session held open across the engagement. Owns FRs, ambiguity arbitration, ADRs. Launch with `claude --agent architect`.
- **Developer** — invoked as a subagent inside an implementation session. Writes code under `src/`. Cannot write tests.
- **Reviewer** — invoked as a subagent inside an implementation session. Verifies `@covers` traceability, runs tests, returns findings. Cannot write production code.

Roles coordinate through artifacts (FR markdown + AC IDs + git PRs), never through chat history. Read the canonical role definitions in `.agent-team/roles/` for full responsibilities and boundaries. The `.claude/agents/` files are thin Claude Code wrappers that point to those role definitions.

## Repository conventions

- Specs live in `specs/` as `FR-XXXX-<slug>.md` with YAML frontmatter (see `specs/_template/`).
- Acceptance criteria carry stable IDs (`AC-1`, `AC-2`, …) so tests can link via `@covers FR-XXXX:AC-Y`.
- Source tags `@implements FR-XXXX` are required on production code; the indexer validates reverse-tag consistency.
- Run `python scripts/index-specs.py` after spec edits to refresh `specs/INDEX.md`.
- Run `python scripts/deploy-gate.py` before merging anything marked `deployed`.

## Glossary

> **[FILL IN]** — Three to five terms specific to this engagement that the agent will encounter. Example: "Pathways = Nova Scotia primary care system; HRM = NS document repository; CDA = Clinical Document Architecture."

## What lives where, briefly

```
specs/                  # FRs (one per stakeholder-noticeable capability)
src/                    # Implementation. @implements tags required.
tests/                  # Tests. @covers FR-XXXX:AC-Y tags required.
scripts/                # index-specs.py, deploy-gate.py, agent-status.py
.agent-team/roles/      # Canonical role definitions (architect/developer/reviewer)
.claude/agents/         # Claude Code subagent wrappers
.claude/settings.json   # Hooks (validation, traceability, PHI regex)
```

## When in doubt

- Ambiguous spec → escalate to Architect (not human, unless Architect is also unsure)
- Missing test for AC → Reviewer self-remediates
- Anything PHI-shaped that wasn't expected → stop, flag to human
- Three Dev↔Reviewer cycles on one PR → escalate to human; the spec is probably wrong
